the nhs testing traces a government-run  service for the whole of the uk  and it's a crucial part of the country's  coronavirus strategy  it's divided into four pillars the main  pillars  are one and two which are nhs tests  and community lab tests and these make  up the bulk of  tests in the uk here  is the official graph used to show the  number of tests  versus the capacity that is available  the gray line here shows the capacity  available in labs from pillar one and  pillar two  the bars and the yellow line here  show the total and average number of  tests processed  now according to the government's chart  capacity  is outstripping the number of tests but  if you examine the data in detail things  look very different  look closely at the pillar to data  here on the 23rd of august you can see  that capacity was breached  that's more people needing a test than  the system  can cope with earlier i spoke to dr  duncan robertson  it's his analysis that found the problem  i asked him what impact this extra  demand on pillar one  could actually have what we're seeing in  the data is that  the number of uh tests in pillar one is  reaching about eighty over eighty  percent  so this means that uh you know a  significant amount of the capacity  within these nhs and public health  england labs is being taken up  and while pillar two is important for  people going to school and getting about  their general life  the real problem comes if you don't have  the capacity  to test patients in the nhs patients and  doctors  so we know that the government's test  and trace system has been overrun but  that hasn't stopped  prime minister boris johnson from  repeatedly claiming  that the uk is carrying out more tests  than any other european country but  is this true well it depends on what  data  you're looking at the latest statistics  show that the uk is carrying out 2.55  tests  a day per thousand people  it is true this is higher than most  countries in europe there's only a few  countries doing more tests than this  like denmark which is doing 6.21 tests  per thousand according to this data  germany  is doing 1.8 tests per thousand it's  crucial to remember though  that the number of tests does not refer  to the same thing  in each country there is no rule as to  what countries need to report whether  it's  number of tests or number of people  tested in fact  in germany they don't even collect data  for the entire country  only by region so the pms claim that we  are doing more  testing than most of europe may not  be entirely true
 

